 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+Eviction uses 2Q — two queues over the one cache budget — so a long sequential screening reclaims its own one-use sheets instead of the smaller comparison set editors keep re-requesting. Entries occupy exactly one queue:
+
+- **Probationary queue (FIFO).** A sheet enters here when its render completes. It leaves on eviction, oldest first, when the queue is over its share of the budget.
+- **Protected queue (LRU).** A sheet in the probationary queue moves here when a later request is served from cache. Each cache hit refreshes its recency position.
+
+A request that joins a pending render job is a cache miss, not a hit: it neither promotes nor refreshes an entry. Only serving a cached JPEG counts as reuse.
+
+The 12 GiB budget is shared between the queues by a tunable split (see Decisions & Alternatives). On admission the worker holds the cache within 12 GiB by evicting the oldest probationary sheet first; it evicts the least-recently-used protected sheet only when the probationary queue cannot meet the bound or the protected queue is over its share. Across the whole cache this reclaims one-use screening sheets before the protected comparison set.
+
+All queue membership, recency, and split state are private to the worker.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q: a FIFO probationary queue plus an LRU protected queue, where a cache hit promotes from probation and refreshes recency | Plain LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | 2Q lets a running screening cycle its one-use sheets through the probationary queue while the comparison set editors re-request stays protected in the second queue. The extra cost is one more queue and a size split, both private to the worker. Plain LRU has the least bookkeeping and adapts fastest when editors switch projects, but a sequential screening can evict the comparison set. Aged frequency counts retain repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and aging, adapt slowly to a project switch, and can hold yesterday's favorites. |
+| Probationary/protected budget split | 25% / 75% | 50% / 50% | A minority probationary share absorbs a screening scan while leaving most of the budget for the reused comparison set. The value is a pilot-tuning constant; see Open Questions. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
